WEBVTT

1
00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.010
If I may. nephew.

2
00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.670
I encountered a situation

3
00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.670
with one of my lieutenants

4
00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.910
at the Stone Mill

5
00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.910
which may have some bearing...

6
00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.380
Why don't you shut your mouth

7
00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.380
about that damned mill?

8
00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.150
And don't call him "nephew." He is your king.

9
00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.650
Robb knows I meant him no...

10
00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.280
You're lucky I'm not your king.

11
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.850
I wouldn't let you wave your blunders around

12
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.850
like a victory flag.

13
00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.660
My blunder sent Tywin's mad dog

14
00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.570
back to Casterly Rock

15
00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.570
with his tail between his legs.

16
00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.970
I think King Robb understands

17
00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.970
we won't win this war

18
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.230
if he's the only one winning any battles.

19
00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.940
- No. there's glory enough to go around.

20
00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.940
- It's not about glory.

21
00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.470
Your instructions were to

22
00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.470
wait for him to come to you.

23
00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.180
- I seized an opportunity.

24
00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.180
- What value was the mill?

25
00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.040
The Mountain was garrisoned

26
00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.040
across the river from it.

27
00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.550
Is he there now?

28
00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.060
Of course not. We took the fight to him.

29
00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.060
He could not withstand us.

30
00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.360
I wanted to draw the Mountain into the west.

31
00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:02.420
into our country.

32
00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:02.420
where we could surround him and kill him.

33
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.730
I wanted him to chase us.

34
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.070
which he would have done

35
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.070
because he is a mad dog

36
00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:10.230
without a strategic thought in his head.

37
00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.330
I could have that head on a spike by now.

38
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.210
Instead. I have a mill.

39
00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:26.320
We took hostages.

40
00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:26.320
Willem Lannister. Martyn Lannister.

41
00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:28.880
Willem and Martyn Lannister are 14 years old.

42
00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.360
Martyn is 15. I believe.

43
00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.200
Tywin Lannister has my sisters.

44
00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:40.800
Have I sued for peace?

45
00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.270
No.

46
00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:46.830
Do you think he'll sue for peace

47
00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:46.830
because we have his father's

48
00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:49.740
brother's great-grandsons?

49
00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:55.980
No.

50
00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:57.950
How many men did you lose?

51
00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:03.150
208. But for every man we lost.

52
00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:03.150
the Lannisters...

53
00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:06.020
We need our men more than Tywin needs his!

54
00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.330
I'm sorry.

55
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.530
- I didn't know.

56
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.530
- You would have.

57
00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:20.010
Right here today at this gathering.

58
00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:20.010
if you had been patient.

59
00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.170
We seem to be running short of patience here.

60
00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.650
You know who isn't?

61
00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.180
Tywin Lannister.

62
00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.400
Intimate. Lovely table.

63
00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:26.330
Better chairs than

64
00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:26.330
the old Small Council chamber.

65
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.200
Conveniently close

66
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.200
to your own quarters. I like it.

67
00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:32.360
What news of Jaime?

68
00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:40.750
20.000 unwashed Northerners

69
00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:40.750
have known about his escape for weeks.

70
00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:45.250
You control more spies and informants

71
00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:45.250
than the rest of the world combined.

72
00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:49.280
Do you mean to tell me that none of you

73
00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:49.280
has any notion of where he is?

74
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.160
- We are trying. my lord.

75
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.160
- Try harder.

76
00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.390
What do we have. then?

77
00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:59.490
Robb Stark and most of his bannermen

78
00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:59.490
are in Riverrun

79
00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:03.330
for the funeral

80
00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:03.330
of his grandfather Lord Hoster Tully.

81
00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:08.370
In Stark's absence.

82
00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:08.370
Roose Bolton holds Harrenhal.

83
00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.940
which would seem to

84
00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.940
make him Lord of Harrenhal.

85
00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.180
- in practice. not in name.

86
00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.180
- Let him have it.

87
00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:18.980
The name suits our purposes

88
00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:18.980
far more than that useless pile of rubble.

89
00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:22.120
The Lord of Harrenhal will make

90
00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:22.120
a worthy suitor for the widow Arryn.

91
00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:25.290
For which I am extremely grateful to you.

92
00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:25.290
my lord.

93
00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.730
Lady Arryn and I have known each other

94
00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.730
since we were children.

95
00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:32.830
She has always been

96
00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:32.830
positively predisposed toward me.

97
00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:38.100
A successful courtship would make

98
00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:38.100
Lord Baelish acting Lord of the Vale.

99
00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:40.970
Titles do seem to breed titles.

100
00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.950
You'll leave for the Eyrie as soon as possible

101
00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.950
and bring Lysa Arryn into the fold.

102
00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.110
Then the young wolf can add his own aunt

103
00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:49.880
to the list of people who have

104
00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:49.880
taken up arms against him.

105
00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:52.940
Far be it from me to hinder true love.

106
00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:57.220
but Lord Baelish's absence

107
00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:57.220
would present certain problems.

108
00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:02.250
The royal wedding may end up being

109
00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:02.250
the most expensive event in living memory.

110
00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:05.230
Summer has ended. hard days lie ahead.

111
00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:09.070
Not a good time

112
00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:09.070
to leave the crown's finances unattended.

113
00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:11.200
Fully agreed.

114
00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:14.400
Which is why I'm naming you

115
00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:14.400
new Master of Coin.

116
00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:16.510
Master of Coin?

117
00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:19.170
It would appear to best suits your talents.

118
00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:20.980
I'm quite good at spending money.

119
00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:24.880
but a lifetime of outrageous wealth

120
00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:24.880
hasn't taught me much about managing it.

121
00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:28.450
I have no doubt

122
00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:28.450
you will prove equal to this challenge.

123
00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:30.320
Hear. hear.

124
00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.490
He lifted her high in the air

125
00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.760
He sniffed and roared and smelled her there

126
00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:38.030
She kicked and wailed. the maid so fair

127
00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.530
But he licked the honey from her hair

128
00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:43.670
From there to here. from here to there

129
00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.870
All black and brown and covered with hair

130
00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.030
He smelled that girl on the summer air

131
00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.600
The bear. the bear. and the maiden fair

132
00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.180
I hope you're pleased.

133
00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.180
If I were armed. they'd never have taken us.

134
00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.240
You were armed when we were taken.

135
00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.220
I was in chains. if you recall.

136
00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:04.090
Our little match would have ended quite quick

137
00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:04.090
if my hands weren't bound.

138
00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.390
All my life I've been hearing.

139
00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.390
"Jaime Lannister.

140
00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:09.160
"what a brilliant swordsman."

141
00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:14.160
You were slower than I expected.

142
00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:14.160
And more predictable.

143
00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:17.690
I've been sitting in a muddy pen.

144
00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:17.690
wrapped in chains. for the past year.

145
00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:19.670
I'm a woman. I was beating you.

146
00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:22.010
You were not beating me.

147
00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.270
Maybe you were as good as people said...

148
00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.280
Once.

149
00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:29.410
Or maybe people just love

150
00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:29.410
to over-praise a famous name.

151
00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:37.680
When we make camp tonight. you'll be raped.

152
00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:37.680
More than once.

153
00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:40.660
None of these fellows

154
00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:40.660
have ever been with a noblewoman.

155
00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.230
- You'd be wise not to resist.

156
00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.230
- Would I?

157
00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:48.130
- They'll knock your teeth out.

158
00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:48.130
- You think I care?

159
00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:50.540
No. I don't think you care about your teeth.

160
00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:58.240
If you fight them. they will kill you.

161
00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:58.240
Do you understand?

162
00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:00.910
I'm the prisoner of value. not you.

163
00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:03.380
Let them have what they want.

164
00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:03.380
What does it matter?

165
00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:05.610
- What does it matter?

166
00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:05.610
- Close your eyes.

167
00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:07.250
Pretend they're Renly.

168
00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:13.890
If you were a woman. you wouldn't resist?

169
00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:13.890
You'd let them do what they wanted?

170
00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:19.230
If I was a woman. I'd make them kill me.

171
00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:19.230
But I'm not. thank the Gods.

172
00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:26.000
If I had a proper forge.

173
00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:26.000
I'd make it good as new.

174
00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:30.410
Doesn't have to look pretty.

175
00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:30.410
It just has to keep the arrows out of my heart.

176
00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:32.410
Why are you helping him?

177
00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:34.610
He takes us prisoner. now he's our friend?

178
00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:36.280
You're not our prisoner.

179
00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:38.620
- What am I?

180
00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:38.620
- Our guest.

181
00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.660
No one's put any chains on you.

182
00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:42.190
So I can walk away. then?

183
00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:45.060
These woods aren't safe

184
00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:45.060
for Ned Stark's daughter.

185
00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:47.390
You're lucky we found you.

186
00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:54.730
You think you're good

187
00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:54.730
with that bow. you little twat?

188
00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:56.270
Better than anyone you've met.

189
00:12:56.700 --> 00:13:00.610
A coward's weapon. I like to fight up close.

190
00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:04.110
I like to see a man's face

191
00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:04.110
when I put the steel in him.

192
00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:06.740
Why? So you can kiss him?

193
00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:08.650
You remember the last time you were here?

194
00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:12.310
Looks like every other shit inn on the road.

195
00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:17.090
Now. apologies. but you're one ugly fucker

196
00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:17.090
and I'd rather not see you no more.

197
00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.660
Watch your head.

198
00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:25.060
Off we go.

199
00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:26.430
Walk up!

200
00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:34.470
What are you doing? We're about to leave.

201
00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:35.870
I'm staying.

202
00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:39.480
You're what?

203
00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:44.080
I baked brown bread for the innkeep.

204
00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:44.080
she said she never had better.

205
00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:48.120
Told Thoros she's keeping me as payment

206
00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:48.120
for all his free meals.

207
00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:52.720
Anyway. it's not me the Brotherhood wants.

208
00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:52.720
My brother ain't no king.

209
00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:54.460
I'm not a Stark of Winterhell.

210
00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:55.790
Winterfell.

211
00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.160
- You sure?

212
00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.160
- I'm sure.

213
00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:04.530
Well. I made you something.

214
00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:10.140
What is it?

215
00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:12.540
It's a wolf.

216
00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:18.950
Yes. it is.

217
00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:21.760
That's the tail?

218
00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:23.220
Yeah.

219
00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:27.620
Well. be safe.

220
00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:31.290
Yeah. you. too. Don't get stabbed.

221
00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:36.000
You don't burn your fingers.

222
00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:47.480
Good-bye. Hot Pie.

223
00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:50.450
Good-bye. Arry.

224
00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:03.760
Up you go. little lady.

225
00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:12.010
Hey. Hot Pie.

226
00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:16.640
It's really good.

227
00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:27.650
A person could almost be forgiven

228
00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:27.650
for forgetting we're at war.

229
00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:34.650
It often comforts me

230
00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:34.650
to think that even in war's darkest days.

231
00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:40.070
in most places in the world

232
00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:40.070
absolutely nothing is happening.

233
00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:45.110
I've missed you. Uncle.

234
00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:48.940
Father missed you. too.

235
00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:48.940
from the day you left.

236
00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:51.240
Maybe he never said it in so many words...

237
00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:52.910
Maybe?

238
00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:55.280
Your father was a stubborn old ox.

239
00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:59.720
I was surprised when he died.

240
00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:59.720
Didn't think death had the patience.

241
00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:06.390
I'm glad you were with him.

242
00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:06.390
I wish to the Gods I had been.

243
00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:12.060
Did you make peace in the end?

244
00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:17.400
After 30 years of fighting.

245
00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:17.400
I don't think he remembered what started it.

246
00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:21.100
He asked me to stop calling myself Blackfish.

247
00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:25.640
He said it was an old joke

248
00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:25.640
and it was never funny to begin with.

249
00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:30.320
I told him people had been

250
00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:30.320
calling me Blackfish for so long.

251
00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.580
they don't remember my real name.

252
00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:40.590
Every time he would leave for the capital

253
00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:40.590
or fight in a campaign.

254
00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:43.860
I'd see him off.

255
00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:46.990
"Wait for me. little Cat." he'd say.

256
00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:52.810
"Wait for me. and I'll come back to you."

257
00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:59.040
And I would sit at this window

258
00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:59.040
every day when the sun came up. waiting.

259
00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:03.920
I wonder.

260
00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:10.050
how many times did Bran or Rickon

261
00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:10.050
stare across the moors of Winterfell.

262
00:17:10.120 --> 00:17:12.460
waiting for me to return?

263
00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:15.760
I will never see them again.

264
00:17:18.330 --> 00:17:20.130
You mustn't think it.

265
00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:25.340
We don't know the truth.

266
00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:25.340
They could be in hiding.

267
00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:30.840
Robb believes they're alive.

268
00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:33.910
And he must go on believing.

269
00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:36.880
He's got to remain strong if he's to prevail.

270
00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:40.750
And you must remain strong for him.

271
00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:53.700
You're Robb Stark's wife.

272
00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:55.960
Hold still.

273
00:17:57.710 --> 00:17:59.540
Is it true what they say about him?

274
00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:03.010
I don't know. What do they say about him?

275
00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.340
That he can turn into a wolf at night.

276
00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:08.080
True.

277
00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:10.410
And he eats the flesh of his enemies.

278
00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:12.350
True.

279
00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:17.320
You're a Lannister. aren't you?

